body:
Write the durable process decision before tooling. Amend ADR 0074 and ADR 0076 or add one focused ADR that defines experience contracts, state matrices, expanded journey budgets, review evidence, exploratory checkpoints, and the boundary between automated proof and human UX judgment.

Acceptance criteria:
- options and tradeoffs are explicit, including what stays advisory versus hard-fail;
- docs/testing.md, docs/ui-authoring.md, docs/dogfooding.md, engineering-workflow DoD, and retro template have one canonical owner for each rule;
- rollout is pilot-first and does not make unfinished infrastructure a universal blocker;
- make check budget and determinism constraints are recorded;
- interaction with existing epics 44beb6e and 0db7a7a is explicit and non-duplicative;
- owner approves the decision before Q1-Q9 enforcement work begins.

Follow-up to v0.47.0 (epic db6be22). The shipped diff shows section rows with +added/-removed counts + status chips, but not WHAT changed. Improve presentation:
- inline-expand a changed section to show the actual line-level text diff (removed red, added green, context dimmed);
- value-aware lines: where a changed line contains numbers, show old -> new (delta) inline;
- filter / group / collapse (Changed / Removed / Added / All), changed-first, collapse the boilerplate tail;
- like-with-like pairing (Q-over-Q, H1-over-H1, annual-over-annual) instead of H1->Q3, which removes most false removed/added noise at the source;
- humanize section labels ('<preamble>' -> 'Wstep', tidy capitalization).
Needs a small backend change: get_report_diff (or a lazy per-section command) returns the actual diff hunks, not just counts. May be hosted by the dockview pilot's layout. Stay narrow-window-safe (no horizontal scroll; ADR 0052 + ui-authoring grid rule).

Validate the complete process on two high-value journeys before making it universal.

Acceptance criteria:
- approved experience briefs, storyboards, and state matrices exist for the touched J1 and J2 slices;
- journey tests run happy, hostile-content, partial-failure, and controlled-async scenarios appropriate to each slice;
- expanded friction metrics and discoverability contracts produce initial baselines;
- visual contact sheets receive explicit owner review;
- both journeys run on the real Windows app and real owner database at vertical-slice and mid-pilot checkpoints;
- escaped findings are classified and drive targeted corrections;
- make check remains green and within the agreed budget;
- retrospective recommends adopt, revise, or reject each new practice before DoD becomes universal;
- owner signs off before epic closure.

Give the guardrail-harvest loop evidence about where defects originate so the response improves planning when planning failed and tests when coverage failed.

Acceptance criteria:
- canonical taxonomy covers spec gap, UX decision, missing state, mock realism, integration seam, responsive layout, visual hierarchy, async race, native runtime, real-data shape, and test flake;
- retro template records each escaped defect once with origin, detection stage, and earlier prevention point;
- a lightweight report summarizes counts and repeated classes without telemetry or vanity targets;
- guidance states that not every escape creates a new automated test;
- two prior retros are classified as a calibration sample;
- repeated classes feed a precise guardrail or an explicit human checklist.

Reduce flaky full-app Vitest workflows and ensure cross-screen behavior is proved at the layer that can observe it.

Acceptance criteria:
- audit classifies existing frontend tests as component state, workflow, layout, journey, visual, or live-runtime proof;
- cross-screen journeys duplicated in full-app Vitest move to Playwright when Playwright is the cheaper authoritative layer;
- Vitest keeps controlled component state, error, form, and async-transition coverage;
- multi-slice implementation contracts name cross-cutting browser specs and full-wave verification before the final gate;
- no coverage behavior is silently removed and the coverage ratchet remains non-decreasing;
- targeted test loop becomes faster or more stable by measured evidence, not by retries.

Make visual inspection cheap enough to happen during implementation instead of leaving screenshots dispersed across snapshot folders.

Acceptance criteria:
- one command generates a review artifact for changed or selected panels across relevant state, pane-size, and theme combinations;
- output uses existing Playwright visual scenarios and baselines rather than a second screenshot framework;
- labels identify screen, state, S-M-L tier, theme, and build stamp;
- artifact is local or short-lived and does not bloat committed history;
- intended baseline updates require a named screen and reason;
- procedure explains that a stable baseline proves change detection, not design quality.

Move real-app exploratory validation earlier than release closure while preserving the final dogfooding walk.

Acceptance criteria:
- docs define a 3-5 minute first-vertical-slice checkpoint, a mid-milestone review, and the existing release dogfood;
- each checkpoint uses a short exploratory charter tied to the served journey, not a scripted happy-path replay;
- live-drive captures build, real database, platform, screenshots, findings, and verdict without collecting telemetry;
- P1 findings block further expansion of the slice; lower-severity friction enters Radicle or the retro honestly;
- Windows native behavior remains the desktop authority;
- one J1 or J2 charter is automated only where automation preserves its purpose, with human judgment explicitly retained.

Extend the existing scenario factory for frontend UX validation without creating a second router or overlapping the generic failure-path epic 0db7a7a.

Acceptance criteria:
- reusable scenario overlays cover hostile text, dense history, partial data, stale processing, conflicting statuses, and mixed Polish and English content;
- controllable promises let tests complete independent commands and jobs in chosen orders;
- overlays compose with empty, minimal, and rich scenarios and remain per-test isolated;
- at least one test proves stale response suppression or equivalent last-intent-wins behavior;
- async failure injection is consumed from 0db7a7a rather than reimplemented;
- scenario guidance states which UI changes require an adversarial pass.

Turn UX-first TDD into reviewable planning artifacts for every non-mechanical panel, screen, or functional redesign.

Acceptance criteria:
- template captures user trigger, desired outcome, decision to make, required evidence, information hierarchy, single primary action, entry/exit path, and done-well criteria;
- storyboard covers entry, before action, loading, success, error, undo or recovery, and narrow-pane behavior;
- state matrix covers empty, loading, partial, success, error, stale, and dense states with justified not-applicable entries;
- approved artifacts live under docs/mockups or the canonical plan, never a session scratchpad;
- feature planning checklist points to the served UX journey and names the first reddening journey test;
- one existing screen is used as a worked example.

Measure common forms of UX friction that a click-only budget misses while keeping hard gates deterministic and low-noise.

Acceptance criteria:
- journey harness can report screen transitions, modal opens, context losses, scroll-before-primary-action, and time-to-visible-feedback using deterministic clocks where required;
- ADR selects which metrics hard-fail, ratchet, or remain review-only;
- budgets are stored and reviewed beside existing interaction floors without duplicating ux-journeys.md intent;
- harness produces actionable failure messages naming the added friction;
- J1 and J2 establish initial measurements;
- viewport-dependent behavior is handled deliberately rather than hidden by forced pane sizing.

Protect whether primary actions are findable and visually coherent, not only whether elements exist.

Acceptance criteria:
- define low-noise contracts for primary-action visibility before scroll, primary CTA count, non-obvious icon-only actions, keyboard focus order, and post-success next-step visibility;
- contracts use semantic roles and design primitives instead of brittle CSS where possible;
- legitimate dense and advanced-layout surfaces have explicit exemptions in canonical docs;
- representative component tests and Playwright checks redden on intentionally introduced violations;
- tests do not attempt to automate subjective visual taste;
- J1 and J2 pilot screens adopt the contracts.
